WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.820 --> 00:00:02.830
Film Courage: What’s your process for approaching
dialogue?

00:00:02.830 --> 00:00:06.180
Erik Bork, Screenwriter/Author: It’s kind
of instinctual I think.

00:00:06.180 --> 00:00:12.179
Once I know what is happening in the scene
and I know where it’s going, where the characters

00:00:12.179 --> 00:00:13.179
enter the scene.

00:00:13.179 --> 00:00:14.419
It’s all about:

00:00:14.419 --> 00:00:15.559
*Whose scene is it?

00:00:15.559 --> 00:00:17.900
*What are they trying to achieve in that scene?

00:00:17.900 --> 00:00:19.830
*What is in the way?

00:00:19.830 --> 00:00:23.439
*What are they doing in trying to get through
whatever is in the way?

00:00:23.439 --> 00:00:27.039
That’s pretty much the template for every
scene.

00:00:27.039 --> 00:00:32.300
Then part of that is going to be dialogue,
part of that is going to be what they say.

00:00:32.300 --> 00:00:39.250
And what they say should be the things that
they would believably say given who they are

00:00:39.250 --> 00:00:44.360
and what their unique voice and way of speaking
is in that situation given what is going on

00:00:44.360 --> 00:00:46.109
and what they want.

00:00:46.109 --> 00:00:49.680
You want to avoid the mistake that people
often make with expositional dialogue where

00:00:49.680 --> 00:00:53.329
people just speak information that the audience
needs to know but they really wouldn’t say

00:00:53.329 --> 00:00:54.840
in that moment.

00:00:54.840 --> 00:00:57.620
You want them to be saying the things that
they would really say.

00:00:57.620 --> 00:01:01.300
When I’m doing it it is kind of instinctual
and I’m just sort of quickly writing what

00:01:01.300 --> 00:01:06.290
I have people say and then maybe go back and
revise it but what is probably in the back

00:01:06.290 --> 00:01:10.970
of my mind is it needs to be what they would
naturally say given who they are and what

00:01:10.970 --> 00:01:11.970
they want.

00:01:11.970 --> 00:01:15.360
And people don’t always express everything
they want, right?

00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:17.020
The best dialogue has subtext.

00:01:17.020 --> 00:01:21.310
Which means the audience can pick up that
they’re thinking and feeling something they

00:01:21.310 --> 00:01:25.670
are not saying but what they are saying is
what they think is the right thing to say

00:01:25.670 --> 00:01:27.979
to try and get whatever it is that they want.

00:01:27.979 --> 00:01:32.800
Or the only thing they are able to say for
whatever reason given who they are and what’s

00:01:32.800 --> 00:01:33.800
going on.

00:01:33.800 --> 00:01:37.990
But what is helpful is there’s a sense of
subtext of we know what the inner life is

00:01:37.990 --> 00:01:41.240
underneath that they are not saying.

00:01:41.240 --> 00:01:49.469
But what they are saying hopefully feels real
for that character and hopefully is entertaining

00:01:49.469 --> 00:01:51.240
and keeping with whatever the genre is.

00:01:51.240 --> 00:01:56.399
I mean if it’s a comedy obviously your dialogue
is going to often be trying to be funny as

00:01:56.399 --> 00:01:58.950
well.

00:01:58.950 --> 00:02:05.670
So yes, it’s not so much of figuring it
out as it’s just an instinctually moving

00:02:05.670 --> 00:02:10.290
through it with those kind of things in the
back of my mind and of course tons of revising,

00:02:10.290 --> 00:02:18.300
going back and rewriting and revising, compressing,
cutting, editing the dialogue over time.

00:02:18.300 --> 00:02:22.900
Film Courage: Are there certain scenes that
stand out that are great for subtext?

00:02:22.900 --> 00:02:25.000
What’s a great study in that?

00:02:25.000 --> 00:02:31.330
Erik: Well Robert McKee in this book STORY
analyzed the scene in CASABLANCA with Humphrey

00:02:31.330 --> 00:02:37.160
Bogart and Ingrid Bergman where he gives you
on the page all of the dialogue in the scene.

00:02:37.160 --> 00:02:42.220
He explains to you what subtext is so that’s
a great thing that stayed with me the most

00:02:42.220 --> 00:02:43.220
from that book.

00:02:43.220 --> 00:02:44.530
It was a really helpful thing.

00:02:44.530 --> 00:02:52.600
There is that scene in ANNIE HALL, the famous
scene where Alvy is at Annie’s apartment

00:02:52.600 --> 00:02:56.232
and he is looking around at the photos on
the wall and he is saying stuff and then the

00:02:56.232 --> 00:03:00.290
subtitles they tell you the subtext which
is like “You are a really attractive girl.”

00:03:00.290 --> 00:03:03.910
But what he’s saying is “That’s a really
interesting use of negative space.”

00:03:03.910 --> 00:03:06.550
And her subtext is “I hope he is not a jerk
like the others.”

00:03:06.550 --> 00:03:07.860
But what she is saying is “Oh really?

00:03:07.860 --> 00:03:09.130
You think so?”

00:03:09.130 --> 00:03:13.850
So it’s a comedic thing but it’s kind
of showing you what subtext is.

00:03:13.850 --> 00:03:19.630
Film Courage: I think Humphrey Bogart was
sort of the master of subtext because so much

00:03:19.630 --> 00:03:26.100
of what he says is sarcasm…but without saying
much there is a look, there is a jab, and

00:03:26.100 --> 00:03:28.770
there a lot of things he’s not overtly saying.

00:03:28.770 --> 00:03:29.880
Erik: Good question.

00:03:29.880 --> 00:03:30.880
I don’t know.

00:03:30.880 --> 00:03:34.040
I mean I don’t know how much credit to give
actors for it because it’s really in the

00:03:34.040 --> 00:03:35.960
writing to some extent.

00:03:35.960 --> 00:03:41.472
You need to see the characters to have that
inner life that is going to be clear to the

00:03:41.472 --> 00:03:47.480
audience but also have dialogue on the page
that is not exactly what you are thinking

00:03:47.480 --> 00:03:49.570
and feeling where you can feel the gap.

00:03:49.570 --> 00:03:55.140
At the same time it’s a big part of acting
to know what am I really feeling and thinking

00:03:55.140 --> 00:03:59.340
because I’m not saying it and what am I
saying and how I portray what I am really

00:03:59.340 --> 00:04:03.230
thinking and feeling to the audience even
though I am sort of hiding it away in my dialogue

00:04:03.230 --> 00:04:07.350
or I am not quite coming out with it in my
dialogue for whatever reason.

00:04:07.350 --> 00:04:10.950
Obviously there are great actors who do that
particularly well.

00:04:10.950 --> 00:04:15.490
Film Courage: Do you think certain decades
of cinema have more or less subtext?

00:04:15.490 --> 00:04:18.100
Erik: I don’t know about that.

00:04:18.100 --> 00:04:24.440
I would say that television writing traditionally
has tended to have less subtext especially

00:04:24.440 --> 00:04:30.350
kind of what we originally think of as Prime
Time Network Dramas and comedies usually have

00:04:30.350 --> 00:04:34.220
a lot of dialogue and the dialogue tends to
be a little more on the surface with just

00:04:34.220 --> 00:04:37.120
saying whatever it is that people are thinking
and feeling.

00:04:37.120 --> 00:04:40.651
Although nowadays when we have all of these
TV shows that are more movie like and then

00:04:40.651 --> 00:04:44.060
they have a different feel to them you might
see a lot of subtext and dialogue there as

00:04:44.060 --> 00:04:45.560
you might do in movies.

00:04:45.560 --> 00:04:51.790
It’s a more like slightly sophisticated
kind of thing which isn’t necessarily present

00:04:51.790 --> 00:04:55.810
in certain genres like if it’s Bruce Willis
in DIE HARD there is probably not a lot of

00:04:55.810 --> 00:04:57.290
subtext right?

00:04:57.290 --> 00:05:02.020
Or if it’s SOUTH PARK, it’s not filled
with subtext right?

00:05:02.020 --> 00:05:11.360
But serious dramas tend to have more subtext
or…I hate to use a pejorative where “it’s

00:05:11.360 --> 00:05:12.730
good if you have it and bad if you don’t.”

00:05:12.730 --> 00:05:17.760
But to some extent that’s true that it tends
to be more interesting and involving if we

00:05:17.760 --> 00:05:20.900
are seeing people who aren’t saying everything
they are really thinking.

00:05:20.900 --> 00:05:23.910
I don’t know if you’ve seen FLEABAG on
Amazon?

00:05:23.910 --> 00:05:27.510
It’s a series that I recently watched both
seasons of.

00:05:27.510 --> 00:05:33.230
It has this great device where the main character
turns to the camera and kind of tells us sometimes

00:05:33.230 --> 00:05:38.231
her subtext and it’s this running dialogue
with the camera which they did a little bit

00:05:38.231 --> 00:05:42.450
of in HOUSE OF CARDS and other shows have
done it too in their own unique way so that

00:05:42.450 --> 00:05:44.070
the audience is in on it.

00:05:44.070 --> 00:05:49.420
She doesn’t give us every bit of subtext
but she gives us some point in looks and moments

00:05:49.420 --> 00:05:52.340
of what she is really thinking but she is
saying something different.

00:05:52.340 --> 00:05:56.550
Film Courage: I mean this in all seriousness,
you think of Bruce Willis in the TV show MOONLIGHTING

00:05:56.550 --> 00:05:59.670
and it has tons of subtext between he the
Sybil Shepherd.

00:05:59.670 --> 00:06:00.780
Erik: Yes, it depends on the genre.

00:06:00.780 --> 00:06:04.370
It’s not about if the actor does or doesn’t
do it, it’s about the project.

00:06:04.370 --> 00:06:10.070
Film Courage: So you don’t think that certain
actors get more roles or are cast in terms

00:06:10.070 --> 00:06:11.070
of this?

00:06:11.070 --> 00:06:14.250
You think of Samuel L. Jackson’s characters
and he is so great in everything he does and

00:06:14.250 --> 00:06:15.350
he’s just going to say what he feels.

00:06:15.350 --> 00:06:19.389
But you know at some point in the film his
character is going to have a mini-meltdown

00:06:19.389 --> 00:06:24.670
and everyone is going to be at his mercy and
they are going to do what he wants.

00:06:24.670 --> 00:06:29.580
Certain actors seem to get roles in more of
one style or another.

00:06:29.580 --> 00:06:34.260
Erik: I mean they may get cast in roles that
are not subtext-ty roles more I suppose but

00:06:34.260 --> 00:06:39.620
I don’t know if certain actors do subtext
and some don’t or some are and some aren’t.

00:06:39.620 --> 00:06:41.330
I think it is more of a writing thing.

00:06:41.330 --> 00:06:44.360
It’s more what kind of scene is it?

00:06:44.360 --> 00:06:48.620
Because we are really talking about scene
level dialogue on the scene level so what

00:06:48.620 --> 00:06:49.670
kind of scene is it?

00:06:49.670 --> 00:06:51.139
Who are the people in the scene?

00:06:51.139 --> 00:06:52.139
What are the agendas?

00:06:52.139 --> 00:06:57.240
What are they trying to communicate or achieve
and what are they really thinking and feeling

00:06:57.240 --> 00:07:01.530
and are those things going to be in the dialogue
or not kind of just depends on the kind of

00:07:01.530 --> 00:07:02.830
genre and kind of scene.

00:07:02.830 --> 00:07:06.300
Film Courage: So Aaron Sorkin maybe less subtext?

00:07:06.300 --> 00:07:08.530
Gillian Flynn maybe more with GONE GIRL?

00:07:08.530 --> 00:07:09.889
Erik: That’s a good point.

00:07:09.889 --> 00:07:16.450
Aaron Sorkin I think is less subtext because
he tends to write these very smart people

00:07:16.450 --> 00:07:19.080
articulately laying out arguments for things.

00:07:19.080 --> 00:07:22.170
I think of THE WEST WING classically that
way.

00:07:22.170 --> 00:07:26.910
So yes, it’s not subtext-ty for the most
part.

00:07:26.910 --> 00:07:31.010
It’s very come out and say your opinion
and argue it with somebody.

00:07:31.010 --> 00:07:34.770
It’s kind of an intellectual cerebral type
of writing.

00:07:34.770 --> 00:07:37.970
I think it’s engaging on that level.

00:07:37.970 --> 00:07:40.430
It’s entertaining on that level.

00:07:40.430 --> 00:07:44.260
I’m not saying that he doesn’t ever have
scenes that have subtext.

00:07:44.260 --> 00:07:45.260
I’m sure he does.

00:07:45.260 --> 00:07:50.090
If you look at THE SOCIAL NETWORK I could
probably imagine scenes that have a lot of

00:07:50.090 --> 00:07:58.930
subtext where Justin Timberlake is on the
phone with Eduardo and he is saying one thing

00:07:58.930 --> 00:08:00.740
but we know what he really means.

00:08:00.740 --> 00:08:04.650
There is stuff that Aaron never does or can’t
do it’s just the style he is most known

00:08:04.650 --> 00:08:08.020
for like from THE WEST WING I think you’re
right, it is less subtext-ty.

